---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "How Much Does an Echelon Treadmill Cost? (2026): Every Model";
const description =
  "Echelon treadmills cost $1,399.99 to $2,195.99 at Echelon on 26 Nov 2026, walking pads from $299.99. Premier adds $39.99 a month or $399.99 a year.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "How Much Does an Echelon Treadmill Cost?", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/how-much-does-an-echelon-treadmill-cost/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does an Echelon treadmill cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On Echelon’s store on 26 November 2026, the Stride-6 was $1,399.99, the Stride-6s $1,599.99, the Stride 50 RCX-22 $1,999.99 and the Stride-8s $2,195.99, against compare-at prices of $1,999.99 to $2,999.99. Echelon’s walking pads were $299.99 (DuoPad) and $399.99 (DuoTread).",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is the Echelon membership for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Echelon Premier was $39.99 a month after a 30-day free trial, $399.99 for a year or $699.99 for two years on Echelon’s treadmill pages on 26 November 2026. The yearly and two-year plans include free shipping on the treadmill. Over five years Premier costs about $1,800 to $2,400.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use an Echelon treadmill without a membership?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Echelon’s Stride-6 page says no membership is needed to run and manual workouts from the console always work, and the Stride-8s page says Freestyle mode and built-in programs are always available. Without Premier you lose the classes, scenic and gamified content, and the five-year parts and labour cover.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Echelon charge for delivery?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Stride-8s includes delivery in the continental US with or without a membership. On the Stride-6, Stride-6s and Stride 50 RCX-22, shipping is free with a 1- or 2-year Premier plan; otherwise Echelon says rates are calculated at checkout. Premium in-home delivery and setup is $299.99 where available.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What warranty comes with an Echelon treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For Stride treadmills bought on or after 4 November 2026, Echelon lists a lifetime motor warranty for the original purchaser, 10 years on the frame, 2 years on parts and 1 year on labour. Active Premier members get parts and labour extended to five years in total.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is Echelon cheaper than Peloton?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually, once the membership is counted. In Black Friday 2026 sales, the Stride-6s was $1,599.99 against $1,995 for Peloton’s Tread Flex, and Premier’s yearly plan costs about $1,000 less over five years than All-Access at $49.99 a month. The Stride-8s costs about $200 more to buy than a Tread Flex but about $800 less over five years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Echelon treadmill cheaper on Amazon?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Sometimes. On 26 November 2026 Amazon matched Echelon on the Stride-6 at $1,399.99 and was cheaper on the Stride-8s ($1,999.99) and DuoTread ($299.99). The Amazon Stride-8s listing states a 300 lb capacity against 400 lb on Echelon’s current model, and Echelon only accepts returns on items bought from echelonfit.com.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-26",
      dateModified: "2026-11-26",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/how-much-does-an-echelon-treadmill-cost/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-26",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-26",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "how much does an echelon treadmill cost",
      "echelon treadmill price",
      "echelon stride 6 price",
      "echelon stride 8s price",
      "echelon premier membership cost",
      "echelon treadmill black friday",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Echelon Prices by Model", link: "#price-table" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">By Price</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Price Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">How Much Does an Echelon Treadmill Cost?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        An Echelon treadmill costs $1,399.99 to $2,195.99 on Echelon’s own store in its Black Friday sale on 26 November 2026: $1,399.99 for the Stride-6, $1,599.99 for the Stride-6s, $1,999.99 for the Stride 50 RCX-22 and $2,195.99 for the Stride-8s. Its walking pads start at $299.99, and the optional Premier membership adds $39.99 a month or $399.99 a year. Here is every model’s price, what works without the membership, the five-year cost and how Echelon compares with Peloton and NordicTrack at the same money.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 26, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on retailer and maker listings, with prices checked on the dates shown.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#price-table" class="btn-ghost">Prices by Model</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Echelon sells its Stride treadmills mainly through its own store, where nearly every model carries
          a crossed-out “compare-at” price and a lower sale price beside it. Behind the sticker sit three
          costs that decide what an Echelon really costs you: delivery, which is free on some models and
          only with a membership on others; the Premier membership, which is optional but drives the
          warranty; and the choice between buying from Echelon or from Amazon, where the same name can
          mean a different version. We read Echelon’s US store and Amazon on 26 November 2026, the day
          before Black Friday. Earlier prices come from our Echelon guide, with the date they were seen.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#price-table">Echelon Treadmill Prices by Model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#each-model">What Each Step Up Costs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-pads-other">Walking Pads, Doorbusters, Open-Box and Older Models</a></li>
            <li><a href="#membership-cost">The Premier Membership on Top</a></li>
            <li><a href="#total-cost">The 5-Year Cost of Each Echelon Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery-warranty">Delivery, Assembly, Returns and Warranty</a></li>
            <li><a href="#vs-rivals">Echelon vs Peloton and NordicTrack at the Same Price</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-cheapest">When and Where an Echelon Treadmill Is Cheapest</a></li>
            <li><a href="#worth-it">Is an Echelon Treadmill Worth the Money?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A new Echelon treadmill costs $1,399.99 to $2,195.99 on Echelon’s store on 26 November
            2026, against compare-at prices of $1,999.99 to $2,999.99, and the optional Premier membership
            adds $39.99 a month or $399.99 a year.</strong> Echelon says every Stride runs in manual mode
            without a membership; the classes, and the longer warranty, need Premier.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Stride-6 (auto-fold, no screen):</strong> $1,399.99 at Echelon ($1,999.99
            compare-at) and $1,399.99 on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Stride-6s (auto-fold, 10-inch touchscreen):</strong> $1,599.99 at Echelon
            ($2,299.99 compare-at). Not found on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Stride-8s (22-inch touchscreen, 400 lb rating):</strong> $2,195.99 at Echelon with
            free delivery ($2,999.99 compare-at), or $1,999.99 on Amazon for a listing that states a
            300 lb capacity.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking pads:</strong> the DuoPad at $299.99 and the DuoTread at $399.99 at Echelon;
            the DuoTread is $299.99 on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>On top:</strong> Premier at $39.99 a month, $399.99 a year or $699.99 for two years,
            which is about $1,800 to $2,400 over five years. The 1- and 2-year plans include free
            shipping.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical answer for most buyers is that a Stride-6s costs about $1,600 to buy this week
            and about $3,600 to own for five years with a yearly Premier plan. For the brand, the fold and
            what each model is like to live with, see our <a href="/echelon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Echelon treadmill guide</a>;
            for the same arithmetic on Echelon’s biggest rival, our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-peloton-tread-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a Peloton treadmill costs</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Echelon Treadmill Prices by Model</h2>
<p>
            The table lists every treadmill in Echelon’s US Stride collection on 26 November 2026, with the
            sale price and the compare-at price Echelon showed, and the Amazon price for the matching
            listing where we found one. Echelon badged the Stride-6, Stride-6s and Stride-8s as Black
            Friday deals; the site banner read “Biggest Sale of the Year. Price guaranteed through Dec 2.”
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it is</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Echelon (26 Nov)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Echelon compare-at</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Amazon (26 Nov)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride-6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Auto-fold, LED console and device holder, 20.5 x 60 in., 330 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399.99 (30% off)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride-6s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride-6 with a 10 in. touchscreen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599.99 (30% off)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,299.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not found</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride 50 RCX-22</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fold-up, 22 in. screen, 22 x 57 in., 15% incline, 330 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999.99 (13% off)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,299.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not found</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride-8s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 in. swivel touchscreen, 20 x 60 in., 400 lb, flat after removing two screws</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,195.99 (26% off), delivery included</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,999.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999.99 (listing states 300 lb)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride DuoTread</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking pad that converts to a compact runner, manual incline to 4.1%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$399.99 (50% off)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$799.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stride DuoPad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking pad, 0.6–4.0 mph, 265 lb, no incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299.99 (50% off)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$599.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not found</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Prices are before sales tax. Specifications are Echelon’s own, from each product page on 26
            November 2026. Three notes keep the table honest. First, Echelon’s Stride-6s page carried two
            prices: the buy box and the store’s product data said $1,599.99, while a comparison box lower
            down the same page still showed $1,799.99, the price we recorded on 20 October; trust the
            figure in the cart. Second, the Amazon listings do not always match Echelon’s current
            specification. Amazon’s Stride-8s listing states a 300 lb capacity and 12.5 mph, where
            Echelon’s 2026 Stride-8s page says 400 lb and 12 mph, and Amazon’s Stride-6 title mentions an
            HD touchscreen that Echelon says the Stride-6 does not have. Third, Echelon’s compare-at prices
            are its own reference figures, not prices we have seen it charge; we have only ever seen
            these models sold below them.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Stride-4s-22 also appeared in the collection at $1,399.99 (compare-at $2,599.00) but was
            marked sold out, so it is not in the table. We found no Stride-10s on Echelon’s US store or on
            Amazon; the only “Stride 10” we found was the Stride 10 Sport, a marketplace listing at
            $399.99 that was also unavailable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="each-model" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Each Step Up Costs</h2>
<p>
            The Stride range is not evenly spaced. At Echelon’s Black Friday prices, the Stride-6s is $200
            more than the Stride-6, and the Stride-8s is $596 more than the Stride-6s. What each step buys,
            from Echelon’s product pages on 26 November 2026:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stride-6 to Stride-6s ($200 more).</strong> Echelon describes the Stride-6s as “the
            Stride-6 with one difference”: a built-in 10-inch HD touchscreen. Both are 2026-model
            treadmills with a DC 3.0 brushless motor, a 20.5 by 60-inch waterfall deck, 12.5 mph, 12 levels
            of power incline to 12.5%, a 330 lb maximum user weight and the one-touch auto-fold. The
            Stride-6 has an LED console and an extra-large device holder, so you run classes from your own
            phone or tablet through the free Echelon Fit app. If you already own a tablet of 10 inches or
            more, the $200 buys convenience rather than a better picture. Echelon’s Stride-6s page also
            says it arrives fully assembled and that, with Premier, Echelon Worlds can change the incline
            to match the terrain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stride-6s to Stride-8s ($596 more).</strong> The Stride-8s swaps the 10-inch screen
            for a 22-inch touchscreen that tilts, swivels and rotates 180 degrees for off-treadmill
            classes, adds trackball controls with 0.1 or 1 mph steps, a wireless charging pad and
            DuroFlex cushioning, and raises the maximum user weight to 400 lb on Echelon’s current page. It
            gives up the auto-fold: Echelon says it folds flat only after removing two screws. Its deck is
            slightly narrower, at 20 by 60 inches, and Echelon lists 12 mph rather than 12.5. It is also
            the only Stride with delivery included whether or not you take a membership, which matters
            more than it sounds (see delivery below).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Stride 50 RCX-22 ($1,999.99).</strong> The odd one out: a conventional fold-up
            treadmill with a 22-inch screen, a wider but shorter 22 by 57-inch deck, 15% incline, 12.5 mph
            and a 330 lb rating, on Echelon’s listing. On our 20 October check it was $1,499.99; on 26
            November it was $1,999.99, badged “New”, with its motor now described as 3.0 peak DC brushless.
            It is the one Echelon treadmill whose price went up into Black Friday, so it is not the bargain
            its “$300 off” badge implies. A 57-inch deck is short for running; taller runners should price
            the 60-inch models.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How prices moved since October</h3>
          <p>
            On 20 October 2026 our <a href="/echelon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Echelon guide</a> recorded the Stride-6 at
            $1,699.99, the Stride-6s at $1,799.99 and the Stride-8s at $2,249.99. By 26 November they were
            $300, $200 and $54 lower, and the Stride-6 and Stride-6s had become 2026 models with a larger
            motor rating and a 330 lb limit, up from 300 lb. The cut on the Stride-8s is small; the bigger
            changes on it are the 400 lb rating and free delivery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads-other" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Pads, Doorbusters, Open-Box and Older Models</h2>
<p>
            Echelon sells more than the four Stride treadmills, and some of the lowest prices on its store
            are for machines that are a different kind of product. These are the ones a shopper searching
            for an Echelon treadmill is likely to see.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stride DuoTread, $399.99 at Echelon, $299.99 on Amazon.</strong> A walking pad with a
            folding support bar that turns it into a compact treadmill, with a detachable remote, manual
            incline up to 4.1% and a frame under 5 inches high. Walking mode runs from 0.6 to 3.7 mph. The
            top speed is where the sources disagree: Echelon’s page says running mode reaches 12.5 mph,
            while the Amazon listing says 7.5 mph, so we do not rely on either. Echelon’s page also says it
            does not connect to the Echelon Fit app, so Premier adds nothing to it as a treadmill. On these
            figures Amazon was $100 cheaper on 26 November.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stride DuoPad, $299.99 at Echelon.</strong> A flat walking pad with a dual-sided deck,
            one side textured as a “recovery” surface. Echelon lists a 1.8 CHP motor, 0.6 to 4.0 mph, no
            incline, a 39-inch running surface, a 265 lb maximum user weight, 46 lb of machine and a
            one-year residential warranty. For how these prices sit against other brands, see our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-walking-pad-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a walking pad costs</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black Friday doorbusters.</strong> Echelon’s Black Friday collection included a Stride
            40 Sport at $449.99 (compare-at $899.99), with the regular listing at $693.93, and the Stride
            Cross Trainer Treadmill at $296.99. Both are listed by Echelon as marketplace or partner
            products, and neither page gave a specification we could check, so we cannot tell you what you
            would be getting. Amazon’s Stride 40 Sport listing was $819.27 and its bullets contradict
            themselves on speed and screen size. Ask before buying either.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Certified open-box.</strong> Echelon says open-box units carry the same limited warranty
            as new. On 26 November the open-box Stride-8s was $1,799.99, $396 below new. The open-box
            Stride-6 ($1,299.99) and Stride-6s ($1,599.99) were sold out, and the open-box Stride-6s was
            priced the same as a new one in the Black Friday sale, which shows how far the new prices
            have fallen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Older and commercial models.</strong> Amazon still listed the original Echelon Stride
            at $1,299.99 and the Stride-s at $1,258.44 on 26 November. Both listings describe a 20 by
            55-inch deck, 12 mph and 10% incline, and the Stride-s listing quotes a 12-month limited parts
            and labour warranty and says the unit needs a Wi-Fi update before it can be used. Those are
            older, smaller machines selling for close to a new 2026 Stride-6, which has a 60-inch deck. At
            the top, Amazon listed the commercial Stride 9S at $5,999.99, and Echelon’s store data showed a
            Stride-9s Pro at $6,399.99; that is gym equipment, outside the scope of this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Premier Membership on Top</h2>
<p>
            Echelon’s paid tier for its equipment is Premier. On its treadmill product pages on 26 November
            2026 Echelon offered it three ways: a 30-day free trial, “then $39.99/mo. Cancel anytime”; 1-Year
            Access at $399.99; and 2-Year Access at $699.99. Echelon’s upsell box gives the effective
            monthly prices as $33.33 for the yearly plan and $29.17 for the two-year plan. One membership
            covers up to five family profiles. A cheaper FitPass tier, from $11.99 a month on Echelon’s
            membership page, covers off-equipment workouts only; Echelon’s fine print says classes for
            equipment-based workouts are not included in it.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plan and billing</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Per year</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Over 3 years</th>
